 "title": "Why You Never Want to Win an Argument",
 "topic": "communication",
 "summary": "The creator claims that trying to win an argument often means losing the relationship. When you focus on proving people wrong or having the last word, you end up with awkward silence, a broken connection, and the burden of apologizing first.",
 "key_points": [
  "Winning an argument usually means losing the relationship and the person.",
  "If your goal is to prove people wrong, you might win the point but you lose the person.",
  "Arguments eventually burn out, but setting out to win them destroys the relationship.",
  "Having the last word often results in awkward silence and passing each other in the hall.",
  "When you have the last word, you are typically the first person up to apologize."
 ],
 "content": "The creator starts by stating, \"You never want to win an argument, and this is why.\" He explains that when you set out to win an argument or prove people wrong, \"you will often lose the relationship.\" Even if you win the point, \"you will lose the person.\" He claims that an argument eventually burns out. To illustrate, he gives an example where two people are in an argument, one says something really hurtful, and the other leaves or hangs up the phone. He asks, \"What have I won?\" and answers that you have won \"awkward silence\" and passing each other in the hall. He concludes that when you have the last word, \"you are typically first up to apologize,\" which is all you have bought yourself as a prize.",
 "status": "done",
